import{_ as t,o,c as r,a2 as n}from"./chunks/framework.Cdp8-jB6.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"ADR-088 — Downrange DOF + range-control entry guidance (the entry computer flies the flight)","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"adr/ADR-088.md","filePath":"adr/ADR-088.md"}'),a={name:"adr/ADR-088.md"};function s(i,e,l,d,c,g){return o(),r("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[n('<h1 id="adr-088-—-downrange-dof-range-control-entry-guidance-the-entry-computer-flies-the-flight" tabindex="-1">ADR-088 — Downrange DOF + range-control entry guidance (the entry computer flies the flight) <a class="header-anchor" href="#adr-088-—-downrange-dof-range-control-entry-guidance-the-entry-computer-flies-the-flight" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;ADR-088 — Downrange DOF + range-control entry guidance (the entry computer flies the flight)&quot;">​</a></h1><blockquote><p>Status · Accepted · 2026-08-31 · Operator-directed (Physics Lab spaceflight program) · builds on ADR-087 (kernel systems layer) + RFC-036 (2-DOF descent) · closes the &quot;computer we teach but don&#39;t use&quot; gap for re-entry lift-steering</p></blockquote><p><strong>Gating sentence.</strong> The planar descent integrator (<code>descent-physics.ts</code>) gains a <strong>downrange degree of freedom</strong> and a <strong>range-control bank law</strong> so a lifting capsule&#39;s entry computer solves the constant vertical-lift fraction cos(bank) that lands it at a target downrange — the real Apollo/Orion/Soyuz range-control job. This is what the ADR-087 <code>systems/entry-steering</code> controller steers <em>to</em>: without a downrange state a bank law has no landing point to null, so it was taught in the Lab but flown by nothing. The solve is a bisection over the (measured) monotone range-vs-bank curve; every existing descent profile (no <code>targetDownrangeKm</code>) integrates byte-identically at full lift-up.</p><h2 id="context" tabindex="-1">Context <a class="header-anchor" href="#context" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Context&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The re-entry lift-steering &quot;computer&quot; (<code>systems/entry-steering.ts</code>, ADR-087) was taught in the Lab but exercised by <strong>zero flights</strong>. Investigation found two stacked reasons:</p><ol><li><strong>Data:</strong> no descent profile authored a lift-to-drag, so every capsule — including the Apollo CM (L/D ≈ 0.3) and Soyuz — flew <em>ballistic</em> (~11 g). Fixed by authoring <code>liftToDragRatio</code> on the crewed Earth-return profiles. <ul><li><strong>ENTRY-ANGLE CALIBRATION (Phase 0a — DONE):</strong> the profiles previously used a too-steep <code>flightPathAngleDeg: 4</code> (~2× the real ~1.5° LEO deorbit), inflating peak-g. Re-authored the 29 orbital Earth capsules to <strong>1.5°</strong> (suborbital Mercury-Redstone hops stay 18°). Orbital <strong>ballistic</strong> capsules (Mercury/Vostok/Voskhod) now read ~7.6–7.7 g — matching the flown ~7.8 g. Earlier drafts wrongly cited <em>lunar-return</em> sources (~6–7 g) as &quot;matching the flown value&quot; for these <em>LEO</em>-return flights — corrected (<a href="https://www.faa.gov/sites/faa.gov/files/about/office_org/headquarters_offices/avs/III.4.1.7_Returning_from_Space.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">FAA/NASA reentry data</a>: Apollo 7 LEO peak 3.3 g).</li><li><strong>DRAG-AREA + L/D SOURCING (Phase 1a — DONE; full sourced table in <a href="./../reference/capsule-entry-aerodynamics">docs/reference/capsule-entry-aerodynamics.md</a>):</strong> the lifting capsules read ~7 g not because of the γ-floor (measured: removing it changes nothing at LEO energy) but because <code>entryCdA</code> was ~3× too small. Corrected <code>entryCdA</code> to real/derived drag areas — Apollo/Skylab <strong>BC 313.5 kg/m²</strong> (NASA, sourced → Cd·A 17.7), others geometry-derived (published base diameter × Apollo-calibrated Cd 1.47). Also corrected <code>liftToDragRatio</code> to <strong>sourced</strong> values: Apollo 0.3, Soyuz <strong>0.3</strong> (<a href="https://prokosmos.ru/2025/11/28/kosmicheskij-korabl-soyuz" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">RU sources</a>), Shenzhou <strong>0.2</strong> (<a href="https://news.qq.com/rain/a/20241105A01HC000" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">CN sources</a>), Dragon <strong>0.18</strong> (<a href="https://repository.gatech.edu/server/api/core/bitstreams/39f02e86-13a2-4066-a79d-2a87df7d2ccd/content" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Georgia Tech</a>), Gemini 0.19 (<a href="https://apps.dtic.mil/sti/tr/pdf/AD0856691.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">DTIC AD0856691</a>). Result: lifting capsules now read <strong>~4.2–5.6 g</strong> (was ~7). Flown guided values: Apollo 3.3 g, Soyuz 3–4 g, Shenzhou ≤4 g, Dragon ~4 g — so the model runs <strong>+1 to +2.6 g</strong> above flown (Soyuz 5.6 g is the worst) because our range solve flies a single FIXED bank; real vehicles ACTIVELY modulate bank to hold a g-limit. <strong>MEASURED NEGATIVE RESULT:</strong> wiring the kernel&#39;s own decel-hold controller (<code>bankLiftFraction</code>) does NOT close this — it <em>overshoots</em> (Apollo target 3 g → peak ~6 g, WORSE than the fixed bank&#39;s 4.2 g), because early in entry, with g below target, it digs <em>in</em> to build g. Gain-independent. Holding a target g needs a predictor-corrector (integrate-ahead) controller — a large build, deferred; the residual stands documented, not faked.</li><li><strong>Synthetic vs sourced fields (Phase 0b/1a — DONE):</strong> <code>entryMassKg</code> + <code>liftToDragRatio</code> (all) + Apollo <code>entryCdA</code> are <strong>sourced</strong>; the ~1.5° <code>flightPathAngleDeg</code>, <code>targetDownrangeKm</code>, and non-Apollo <code>entryCdA</code> are <strong>model estimates</strong>, declared per-profile in <code>estimatedFields</code> with a test enforcing that estimates are flagged and sourced fields are not — so an estimate cannot silently wear mission-report provenance.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Model:</strong> the bank <em>controller&#39;s</em> real job is <strong>downrange range control</strong> (steer to the recovery zone) and the lunar-return skip — but the integrator is planar with no downrange state, so a steering law had nothing to steer. A naive decel-hold bank law, applied without a range DOF, only <em>raised</em> peak-g (it modulates lift <em>down</em> from the peak-limiting full-lift-up attitude). That was the honest finding that redirected the work: don&#39;t wire a controller with no job — give the model the dimension the controller acts on.</li></ol><h2 id="decision" tabindex="-1">Decision <a class="header-anchor" href="#decision" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Decision&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Downrange state.</strong> <code>integrateDescent</code> integrates <code>downrange += v·cosγ·dt</code>, exposed as <code>DescentState.downrangeKm</code> (the entry ground track) and <code>DescentSummary.landingDownrangeKm</code>.</p><p><strong>Bank-scaled lift.</strong> The entry γ-integration lift becomes <code>L = (L/D)·D·cos(bank)</code>. <code>cos(bank) = +1</code> is full lift-up (peak-limiting attitude: max range, min g); <code>−1</code> is lift-down (dig in: short range, high g). <code>DescentOptions.entryBankCos</code> carries it; default <code>1</code> preserves the prior full-lift-up behaviour exactly.</p><p><strong>Range-control solve.</strong> A profile with <code>targetDownrangeKm</code> triggers <code>integrateGuidedDescent</code>: downrange is <strong>monotone</strong> in cos(bank), so a 28-step <strong>bisection</strong> finds the bank that lands at the target — no per-case tuning. Out-of-footprint targets clamp to the reachable edge and set <code>guidance.targetReachable = false</code> (honest: the computer reports it cannot reach, it does not fake it). The solved bank + reachability ride on <code>DescentSummary.guidance</code>.</p><p>This is the range-error → commanded-bank architecture of real entry guidance. The measured footprint for the Apollo CM (7.82 km/s, γ 4°) is ~1083 km (lift-down, 24.6 g) to ~1928 km (lift-up, 7.1 g); a ~1500 km target solves to a mid bank at ~7.5 g. NOTE these ranges + g are the <em>steep-4°</em> model, not flown values (flown ~3.3 g, longer range) — Phase 0a re-calibration to ~1.5° is pending.</p><h2 id="consequences" tabindex="-1">Consequences <a class="header-anchor" href="#consequences" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Consequences&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>The entry computer now flies a real flight</strong> — it steers the app&#39;s Earth-capsule descent to a chosen landing point, the same range-target → commanded-bank <em>principle</em> the Lab teaches (NOT the same code: the app&#39;s <code>integrateGuidedDescent</code> and the Lab&#39;s <code>solveEntryBankForRange</code> are separate solvers — reconcile in Phase 0d). Verified: solves to any reachable target; flags the unreachable.</li><li><strong>No behaviour change for the non-lifting profiles</strong> — at ship time the range DOF changed nothing for a profile without <code>targetDownrangeKm</code> (<code>entryBankCos</code> defaults to 1, full lift-up). Since Phase 1a the 16 lifting Earth capsules DO set <code>targetDownrangeKm</code> (they fly the guided solve); the other 59 of the 75 shipped profiles are unaffected. Typecheck clean; descent + HUD suites green.</li><li><strong>A ground-track readout</strong> (<code>downrangeKm</code>) is now available to the <code>/fly</code> descent scene/HUD.</li></ul><h2 id="non-goals-honest-simplifications" tabindex="-1">Non-goals / honest simplifications <a class="header-anchor" href="#non-goals-honest-simplifications" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Non-goals / honest simplifications&quot;">​</a></h2><ul><li><strong>Crossrange / bank reversals</strong> — out of scope. Real guidance banks laterally and reverses to null crossrange; this model is planar, downrange-only.</li><li><strong>Live closed-loop guidance</strong> — the solve is an offline bisection for a single held bank, not Apollo&#39;s stored-reference-trajectory + linearised-gain closed loop flown continuously. The architecture (range target → bank) is faithful; the mechanism is teaching-grade.</li><li><strong>The lunar-return skip</strong> (Apollo 4 double-g-pulse at 11 km/s) — deferred to task #29. It needs the γ-floor removed for the super-circular lifting case plus an exo-atmospheric Keplerian coast to bound Euler energy drift; it belongs with the Apollo round-trip capstone where the 11 km/s entry is the point.</li><li><strong>Mars guided-lifting entries</strong> — Curiosity/MSL (first guided Mars entry), Perseverance, Tianwen-1 flew real lifting, bank-steered entries. NOW modelled 2-DOF (ADR-089 Phase 2): real ~15° FPA + L/D 0.24 (MSL sourced <a href="https://ntrs.nasa.gov/api/citations/20110003649/downloads/20110003649.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">MSL Entry Guidance</a>), ~6.5–8.5 g (flown MSL ~12 g — the fixed-bank residual, since we fly lift-up without the active bank-modulation that digs deeper).</li><li><strong>Not re-tuning the Lab entry-corridor lesson</strong> — the corridor-widening claim (lift roughly doubles the survivable entry-angle band) stands unchanged.</li></ul>',15)])])}const f=t(a,[["render",s]]);export{u as __pageData,f as default};
